Üƒ±ÿË"ÑWhen connecting the Subtiplayer app to Anki using the AnkiConnect add-on, I encountered a CORS error. To fix this issue, I had to slightly modify the add-onâ€™s code. Unfortunately, I was unable to contact the original author, so I temporarily published the fixed version as AnkiConnect Fixed (https://ankiweb.net/shared/info/734459859).
®¡üòË"£People with issues due to App Nap in recent Mac OS may try my fork at lower_cpu_usage_qtcpserver branch

https://git.sr.ht/~self-perfection/anki-connect/tree/lower_cpu_usage_qtcpserver

It replaces polling on timer, which produced constant small CPU load, with event-driven I/O. I do not know anything about MacOS and do not use it, but _probably_ it will not freeze background app if it does not use CPU in background.
­«Ã…Ë"¢To disable App Nap on Mavericks, I followed the suggestion on this comment: https://forums.ankiweb.net/t/recent-anki-updates-bundle-id-change-disabling-app-nap-macos-anki-connect/65550/7
and added this code to __init.py__

from aqt._macos_helper import macos_helper as h
h.disable_appnap()

¼·Á‰Ê"±(answering a few of the recent questions)
On my webapp, I needed to use 127.0.0.1:8765 and NOT localhost:8765 - as chrome seems to try IPv6 first, and AnkiConnect only listens on IPv4. (at least, I think this is what fixed it for me!)
The 'new' homepage seems to be https://git.sr.ht/~foosoft/anki-connect
